{"qid":"Q1150344","names":["Sultanate of Johor"],"title":"Sultanate of Johor","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Sultanate of Johor was founded in 1528 by Alauddin Riayat Shah II, a son of the last Sultan of Malacca, after the Portuguese seized Malacca in 1511. At its peak the sultanate controlled the southern Malay Peninsula, Singapore, and portions of the Riau archipelago and eastern Sumatra, positioning itself as the legitimate heir of Malaccan commercial and political prestige. The polity navigated repeated Dutch and Portuguese military pressure, concluding a treaty with the Dutch East India Company (VOC) in 1641 that together expelled the Portuguese from Malacca; the VOC alliance also constrained Johor's sovereignty. The 1885 Treaty of Friendship with Britain effectively ended Johor's independence, and the subsequent Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1824 had already split its insular territories into the separate Riau-Lingga Sultanate.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The sultanate's core peninsular territory is well documented, but the precise extent of its offshore island and Sumatran claims varied substantially by period and is imprecisely mapped.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Johor sultanate","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Johor-sultanate"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Johor Sultanate","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johor_Sultanate"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Johor Sultanate","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Alauddin Riayat Shah II","nativeName":"علاء الدين رعايت شاه","roman":"ʿAlāʾ al-Dīn Riāyat Shāh II","start":1528,"end":1564,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLice